{"number": 201, "text": "                and         but         or       so_      because\n\n\n         A\n                   and     but     or    so    because\n                  We  use these words (conjunctions) to join two sentences. They make one longer sentence from two\n                  shorter sentences:\n                               sentence A                       The  driver got out. sentence B\n                                           The car stopped, and the driver got out.\n\n\n            4\n         B       and/but/or\n                               sentence A         sentence B\n\n                         We  stayed home   and    (we)* watched TV.        *It isnot necessary to\n                       My sister is married                    Houston.  |  repeat “we” and “she.”\n                       He doesn’t like her, and   she doesn't like him.\n                     | bought a sandwich,  but  — | didn’t eat it.\n                                           but    it doesn’t have a garage.\n                   Do you want  to go out, or     are you too tired?\n\n                 In lists, we use commas (,). We use and before the last thing:\n                              home,  had something  to eat, sat down in an armchair, and fell asleep.\n                                                                               a\n                      ©.  Karen is at work, Sue is shopping, and Chris is playing football.\n                 so  (the result of something)\n                                sentence A       sentence B\n\n                           It was very hot, so   | opened the window.\n                   Joe playsalot of sports, so   he's very fit.\n                   They don't like to travel, so they haven't been to many places.\n          :   |   because  (the reason for something)\n                                   sentence A             sentence B\n\n                                  the window    because __ it was very hot.\n     